---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/tcpa';
const title = 'TCPA Basics for SMS Campaign Marketers | Shopify SMS Marketing';
const desc = 'Separate federal SMS rules, applicable state law and provider policy, then build consent evidence, suppression and dispatch controls for Shopify campaigns.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/guides" class="link text-sm">← All SMS guides</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Consent and sending controls</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">TCPA Basics for SMS Campaign Marketers</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A customer profile, purchase or waitlist entry is not enough to decide whether a promotional text may be sent. Establish the applicable rules, preserve the permission evidence and check current suppression before dispatch.</p>
      <p class="mt-6">Legal sources reviewed November 25, 2026. General information, not legal advice or a compliance guarantee. Have counsel apply the rules to your audience, technology, sender relationships and message content.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-8">
      <h2>Separate the rule layers before configuring a flow</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/section-64.1200">47 CFR § 64.1200(a)(2)</a> requires prior express written consent for covered advertising or telemarketing calls using an automatic telephone dialing system or artificial or prerecorded voice. Do not turn that technology-dependent rule into a claim that every text has the same federal consent requirement. Separate do-not-call rules in paragraphs (c), (d) and (e) also matter for marketing texts to wireless numbers. Counsel should review coverage and any claimed exception.</p>
      <p>State law needs its own review. For example, <a class="link" href="https://www.leg.state.fl.us/statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&amp;URL=0500-0599/0501/Sections/0501.059.html">Florida Statutes § 501.059</a> defines telephonic sales calls to include texts and contains its own definitions, consent provisions and exceptions. That is an example, not a complete state survey or a rule for every recipient. Identify the applicable jurisdictions rather than relying on the store address.</p>
      <p>Carrier and platform policies are another layer. Obtain the current policy for your sender type, provider and destination market; record its version and effective date. Registration, a vendor consent flag or a successful delivery does not establish legal permission. Treat any stricter provider rule as a separate dispatch constraint.</p>
      <h2>Do not build around the vacated one-to-one rule</h2>
      <p>On January 24, 2025, the Eleventh Circuit in <a class="link" href="https://media.ca11.uscourts.gov/opinions/pub/files/202410277.pdf">Insurance Marketing Coalition v. FCC, No. 24-10277</a> vacated Part III.D of the FCC's 2023 order, including its one-to-one and logically/topically associated consent restrictions. The court expressly noted that the earlier 2012 rule was not at issue. This does not make an imported lead or a vague partner disclosure sufficient. Review the actual agreement, seller identity and proposed use with counsel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Revocation: account for the November 2026 change</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-26/pdf/2026-21821.pdf">FCC final rule published October 26, 2026, effective November 25, 2026</a> revises § 64.1200(a)(10)–(12). Informational revocation may be limited to its category; a telemarketing revocation reaches future advertising and telemarketing calls and texts from that caller. It is not a universal instruction to unsubscribe every email channel.</p>
      <p>The rule permits specified exclusive revocation methods with clear disclosure. A sender using reply texts must recognize all listed standardized words, not just STOP. Without a designated exclusive method, reasonable means remain available. Covered requests must be honored within a reasonable time, no more than ten business days; this ceiling is not permission to keep campaigns running until then. Confirmation texts have separate conditions, including no promotional content.</p>
      <p>Have counsel approve the chosen method, scope and disclosures. As an operating control, block promotional dispatch on receipt while synchronizing suppression across senders and tools. Route ambiguous replies and support requests to a monitored owner; do not silently discard them because a keyword parser failed. Verify the provider can implement the approved behavior before launch.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-8">
      <h2>Build a consent evidence packet</h2>
      <p>For each number, preserve the capture source, event time with offset, normalized number, customer identifier, seller and message scope, exact displayed disclosure version, and the affirmative action or signature evidence. Keep the form context and applicable terms versions with the event. These are proposed evidence controls; counsel should determine what proves a valid agreement for your implementation.</p>
      <p>Keep the original record immutable and link corrections rather than rewriting history. Distinguish when an event occurred from when your system received it. If evidence is missing, quarantine the contact from the affected campaign and investigate; do not invent a timestamp or recreate disclosure text from today's popup. A purchase, email subscription or restored Shopify profile should not automatically set an SMS marketing permission flag.</p>
      <h2>Resolve conflicting events before releasing a job</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Store opt-in and revocation events with source, scope, event ID, occurrence time and receipt time. Deduplicate retries without losing the original request.</li>
        <li>Keep suppression effective when an old opt-in arrives late. Do not let the last webhook received automatically win.</li>
        <li>Hold contradictory or unorderable evidence for review. Require evidence of a valid later permission before lifting the relevant suppression.</li>
        <li>Keep number identity separate from customer identity. Profile merges, number changes and suspected reassignment need review; permission should not transfer merely because two records share an email address.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These are proposed conservative controls, not conclusions about which event legally controls a particular dispute. Escalate scope disputes, affiliate questions and claimed contractual restrictions on revocation to counsel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Retire queued work when eligibility changes</h2>
      <p>Check permission scope, suppression, applicable timing rules and provider restrictions again immediately before handing a message to the provider. Audience selection at scheduling time is not enough. On a relevant revocation, retire pending promotional jobs, retry jobs, delayed journeys and cached exports across the tools acting for that caller. Preserve cancellation outcomes and escalate work already handed off or impossible to cancel.</p>
      <p>Use an idempotent job identifier and a recorded eligibility decision so a duplicate webhook or worker retry cannot create a second send. Pause affected dispatch if the suppression feed is stale or unavailable. After recovery, re-evaluate each job; retire expired offers and obsolete cart nudges rather than replaying a backlog. Purchase exits, support holds, frequency caps and holdouts are additional merchant controls, not substitutes for legal permission.</p>
      <h2>Use recipient location and current rules for timing</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/section-64.1200">§ 64.1200(c)(1)</a> sets an 8 a.m.–9 p.m. local-time restriction for covered residential telephone solicitations; paragraph (e) addresses wireless marketing calls and texts. This is not a universal safe sending window. Apply applicable state restrictions and provider policies as well, with counsel resolving coverage and exceptions.</p>
      <p>Document the timezone source and confidence, including travel, daylight-saving transitions and conflicting location data. An area code alone may not locate the recipient. Hold messages when location or timing cannot be resolved under the approved policy. Re-check local time at actual dispatch after a delay; do not release the entire overnight queue simply because the store's clock reaches morning. See the <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours guide</a> for operating questions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-8">
      <h2>Keep records that explain a send or a block</h2>
      <p>Link the evidence packet, message category and rendered content, sender identity, applicable policy version, eligibility decision, queue history, dispatch time, provider response and any later complaint. Preserve revocation and suppression history through migration, including the old sender's pending jobs. Reconcile counts and investigate missing records before activating the replacement system.</p>
      <p>Agree retention periods, access controls, deletion handling and legal holds with counsel and the privacy owner. There is no single retention duration or penalty calculation supplied by this guide. Do not promise a safe harbor because a provider accepted the message, a consent record exists or a checklist passed.</p>
      <h2>Proposed acceptance scenarios before launch</h2>
      <p>Use synthetic contacts and a non-delivering test path to check these cases. They are operational acceptance criteria, not reported merchant results or proof of legal compliance.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A STOP arrives after scheduling but before dispatch: the marketing job and its retries are retired and the decision is recorded.</li>
        <li>An earlier opt-in webhook arrives after a revocation: the contact stays suppressed pending valid evidence and review.</li>
        <li>A timezone cannot be established or a dispatch delay crosses the approved window: the job stays held or is retired when stale.</li>
        <li>A migration imports an active contact already on the suppression ledger: activation is blocked and the mismatch is investigated.</li>
        <li>A provider cannot cancel a submitted job: the incident owner records the limitation, stops further affected work and escalates the exposure.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Assign an escalation owner</h2>
      <p>The marketing owner classifies the proposed message, engineering owns event ordering and dispatch controls, support routes customer requests, and counsel resolves legal coverage and disputed permission. Stop the affected flow when evidence or suppression cannot be trusted. Preserve the incident timeline and investigate the affected audience before restarting; do not send a new promotional text merely to ask whether the customer still consents.</p>
      <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent capture</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/list-growth">list growth</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email–SMS coordination</a>. Use the <a class="link" href="/alternatives">alternatives directory</a> when planning a migration, with evidence and suppression transfer included in the handover.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
